@page "/privacy"
@attribute [AllowAnonymous]

<PageTitle>FairShare - Privacy</PageTitle>

@* Every sentence on this page must stay literally true of the running code (ADR 0003/0004).
   If an implementation change would falsify one, change the sentence in the same PR. *@
<div class="container my-5" style="max-width: 720px;">
    <h1 class="h3 mb-4">Privacy</h1>
    <p class="lead">
        FairShare exists so parents in its supported states can work out a child-support
        number without handing their financial life to anyone - including us.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h5 mt-4">What you type stays yours</h2>
    <p>
        Calculations run against our server, but <strong>nothing you type is stored unless you
        choose to save it</strong>. Guests can calculate and export freely; no incomes, names,
        or case details are kept. If you sign in, the two things you can save - parent profiles
        and scenarios - are stored for your account only. Saved items have no expiry of their
        own: a scenario you delete is removed from the live database at once; removing a saved
        parent archives it out of use; and deleting your account removes everything it ever
        saved, archived parents included. Short-lived backup snapshots exist for disaster
        recovery only - they are never served by the app, and it deletes any older than 30
        days every time it starts or takes a new snapshot, retrying at each start until the
        delete succeeds.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h5 mt-4">Saved scenarios</h2>
    <p>
        A saved scenario stores <strong>the worksheet's inputs exactly as you entered them</strong> -
        every figure on that state's form, whatever it asks for (incomes, costs, overnights,
        children) - together with a snapshot of the computed result. Scenarios are visible to
        your account only - not to other users, and not to administrators.
    </p>
    <p>
        The scenario <em>name</em> is free text you choose. The figures are already inside the
        scenario, so the name never needs to identify anyone - avoid putting anything in it you
        would not want stored, such as a case number or someone's full name.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h5 mt-4">Accounts</h2>
    <p>
        Sign-in uses your Google account. We store your <strong>email address and a Google
        account identifier</strong> so you can sign back in - and nothing else Google knows
        about you (we do not request your name or photo). FairShare never sees or stores a
        password for Google accounts.
    </p>
    <p>
        "Remember this device" is your call: leave it unchecked (the default) and your sign-in
        ends when the browser closes; check it and this device stays signed in for 30 days.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Deleting your account deletes everything it owns</strong> - profiles, sessions,
        the account itself - immediately from the live database, and from backup snapshots as
        those expire (30 days; deleted at the app's next start or snapshot, retried until it
        succeeds). Minimal security
        records (for example, that an account
        signed in or was deleted, with its name at the time) are kept for up to one year and
        then erased; they never contain case content.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h5 mt-4">Counting visits without tracking you</h2>
    <p>
        We count visits with our own code - <strong>no third-party analytics, no advertising
        scripts, and no cookies for tracking</strong>. Your address and browser details are
        never stored; they briefly feed a one-way code that changes every day, so we can see
        "how many people visited today" but <em>cannot</em> recognize you tomorrow, and neither
        could anyone who obtained the data.
    </p>
    <p>
        If your browser sends Do Not Track or Global Privacy Control, we record
        <strong>nothing at all</strong>. What we do record is only events like "a calculation
        was completed" - never the figures in it. Raw records are deleted after 90 days; only
        daily totals remain.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h5 mt-4">Cookies</h2>
    <p>
        Every cookie FairShare sets exists only to sign you in: one keeps your session
        (including guest sessions), and signing in with Google briefly uses short-lived
        cookies to complete that handshake securely. None are used for tracking, none are
        shared with anyone, and there are no advertising or analytics cookies at all.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h5 mt-4">Questions</h2>
    <p>
        This page describes what the code actually does - the source is public at
        <a href="https://github.com/JJWren/FairShare" rel="noopener" target="_blank">github.com/JJWren/FairShare</a>,
        including the decision records that bind us to these behaviors. For questions, open an
        issue at <a href="https://github.com/JJWren/FairShare/issues" rel="noopener" target="_blank">github.com/JJWren/FairShare/issues</a> -
        though deleting anything, your account included, never requires asking anyone.
    </p>

    <p class="text-muted">Last updated: August 28, 2026.</p>
</div>
